      "features": [
        "Emphysema on imaging"
      ],
      "rationale": "Strongest evidence for predicting differences in treatment benefit because effect-heterogeneity support is consistent across multiple validation approaches. Penalized treatment interactions identified a nonzero interaction in all three analyses, the univariable R-learner had positive validation R-loss gains in all three analyses with similar magnitudes (0.003-0.006), and the causal forest showed positive permutation R-loss increases in all three analyses (0.002-0.004). These results indicate that emphysema consistently improved prediction of treatment-effect differences relative to a constant treatment effect. The nominal univariable interaction p-values were mixed (2 of 3 below 0.05; one at 0.06) and unadjusted for multiplicity, so that line of evidence is less definitive, but it does not outweigh the convergent positive findings from the main effect-heterogeneity validation methods. Main-effect prognostic evidence is limited and treatment-related main-effect selection is unavailable, but the key requirement is treatment-effect modification, for which the evidence is strongest among the supplied variables."
    },
    {
      "features": [
        "Serum creatinine"
      ],
      "rationale": "Weaker evidence for predicting differences in treatment benefit despite being associated with prognosis or treatment assignment in penalized main-effect models. Serum creatinine was selected in outcome models in all three analyses and in treatment models in 2 of 3, but this mainly supports prediction of outcome or treatment receipt rather than heterogeneity of treatment benefit. Effect-heterogeneity evidence is inconsistent and generally null: only 1 of 3 penalized interaction analyses selected it; the univariable R-learner showed no positive validation R-loss gains in any analysis (-0.002, 0.0, -0.001); and the causal forest showed a positive permutation R-loss increase in only 1 of 3 analyses, with values near zero (-0.001, 0.001, 0.0). The nominal interaction screen was mixed (p-values 0.02, 0.04, 0.2; 2 of 3 below 0.05) and lacks multiplicity adjustment, providing limited evidence. Overall, serum creatinine may be prognostic, but credible validation evidence that it modifies treatment benefit is weak."
    }
